<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mau Mau rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1952">1952</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mau Mau uprising, Kenya Emergency</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mau Mau rebellion was an armed uprising in colonial Kenya, mainly among the Kikuyu, against British rule and European settlement on African land. The British declared a state of emergency in 1952, detained tens of thousands in camps and killed many thousands of suspected rebels. The emergency ended in 1960, and Kenya became independent in 1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mau-mau-rebellion.jpg" alt="Mau Mau rebellion" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministry of Defence POST-1945 OFFICIAL COLLECTION · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KAR_Mau_Mau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Land was the first British interest in Kenya, which had some of the richest agricultural soils in the world, in districts where Europeans could live permanently. Kenya was claimed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> by a proclamation of 1 July 1895 and declared a colony in 1920. Kikuyu squatters formed most of the labour on settler farms, and from 1918 a series of Resident Native Labourers Ordinances curtailed their rights. The Ordinance of 1939 removed their remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">tenancy</a> rights and let settlers demand 270 days' labour a year.</p><p>Political organisation grew in step. The Kenya African Study Union was founded on 1 October 1944 and became the Kenya African Union in 1946. Many organisers were ex-soldiers who had fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, and who returned to find that they were unpaid and unrecognised, while British counterparts received medals and land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Kenya Land and Freedom Army, called Mau Mau by others, was made up mainly of Kikuyu, Meru and Embu fighters. The origin of the name is uncertain. The British and much international opinion saw it as a savage tribal cult. On 20 October 1952 Governor Evelyn Baring declared a State of Emergency, and Operation Jock Scott arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a> and about 180 other identified leaders. Hundreds, then thousands, of adherents fled to the forests of the Aberdares and around Mount Kenya, where they formed platoons. In May 1953 General George Erskine was sent to restore order.</p><p>The British screened suspects in camps, the system known as the Pipeline, which developed into what has been described as a British gulag. Villagisation concentrated rural people in fortified villages, and the Swynnerton Plan created larger family holdings. The capture of Field Marshal Dedan Kimathi on 21 October 1956 signalled the defeat of Mau Mau and essentially ended the military campaign. Suppression cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> £55 million and at least 11,000 deaths, including 1,090 executions by hanging, with some estimates far higher. The war also set rebels against African loyalists within the Kikuyu community.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In June 1956 a land reform enlarged Kikuyu holdings and the ban on Africans growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> was relaxed. Direct election of African members to the Legislative Assembly followed, and a conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> in January 1960 accepted majority rule. Historians disagree on how far the rebellion hastened independence: a common view holds that continued colonial rule would have needed more force than the British public would tolerate, while others stress the growth of nationalism.</p><p>Mau Mau remained banned until September 2003. On 6 June 2013 the British government announced a settlement with 5,228 claimants, worth £19.9 million, and support for a memorial in Nairobi, while denying liability. Its place in Kenyan memory has been contested since independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mau Mau rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The settler-dominated order set up in these years shaped Kenyan politics up to the <b>Mau Mau uprising</b> and independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-africa-protectorate">East Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>Kenya Colony became independent on 12 December 1963 under Jomo Kenyatta, leader of the Kenya African National Union (KANU), after the <b>Mau Mau</b> emergency and negotiations at Lancaster House.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenya">Kenya</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Mau Mau uprising</b> from 1952 led to a state of emergency under Governor Evelyn Baring and mass detention.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenya-colony">Kenya Colony</a></li></ul></section>
